1562
01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:07.310
and are conducting themselves

1563
01:02:07.310 --> 01:02:12.310
with some level of respectable decorum

1564
01:02:13.810 --> 01:02:16.250
and you find out like, it's just a banana republic

1565
01:02:16.250 --> 01:02:17.758
of corrupt influences.

1566
01:02:17.758 --> 01:02:18.591
Right.

1567
01:02:20.090 --> 01:02:23.800
It is like the 1920s still over there.

1568
01:02:23.800 --> 01:02:26.940
Like whoever, they don't read the report, it's like--

1569
01:02:26.940 --> 01:02:28.030
So, explain the Japan (indistinct).

1570
01:02:28.030 --> 01:02:30.291
So, apparently, July would--

1571
01:02:30.291 --> 01:02:32.080
I was happy.

1572
01:02:32.080 --> 01:02:34.920
We were even talking about going to Tokyo before COVID

1573
01:02:34.920 --> 01:02:36.610
and doing podcasts from there.

1574
01:02:36.610 --> 01:02:39.900
That was gonna be the first thing we collaborated on.

1575
01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:41.280
We both wanted to go to Japan,

1576
01:02:41.280 --> 01:02:43.610
I'm happy that the Olympics are in Japan.

1577
01:02:43.610 --> 01:02:44.630
I think it's awesome.

1578
01:02:44.630 --> 01:02:47.380
But in 1968, when Mexico City got the Olympics,

1579
01:02:47.380 --> 01:02:49.090
they moved the Olympics to early October.

1580
01:02:49.090 --> 01:02:51.240
If I remember correctly, late September, early October,

1581
01:02:51.240 --> 01:02:53.288
because of weather and weather patterns

1582
01:02:53.288 --> 01:02:55.380
and healthy air quality,

1583
01:02:55.380 --> 01:02:56.790
and Japan could have done that.

1584
01:02:56.790 --> 01:02:59.340
Like it could be in September when it's nice there.

1585
01:03:00.344 --> 01:03:03.590
It's late July when it's like 95 degrees

1586
01:03:03.590 --> 01:03:04.713
with 90% humidity.

1587
01:03:07.050 --> 01:03:07.883
And in their bid,

1588
01:03:07.883 --> 01:03:11.060
they characterize the weather as being sort of balmy

1589
01:03:11.060 --> 01:03:14.710
and not nearly as severe as it actually is

1590
01:03:14.710 --> 01:03:17.182
in order to secure the contract.

1591
01:03:17.182 --> 01:03:20.932
And because of the way television works in sports,

1592
01:03:20.932 --> 01:03:23.830
there's no way that they would have pushed it to the fall

1593
01:03:23.830 --> 01:03:25.663
because it competes with football.

1594
01:03:26.800 --> 01:03:30.010
Yeah, back in '68, that wasn't as much of an issue, yeah.

1595
01:03:30.010 --> 01:03:32.720
Yeah, so, it's all about TV, but it's so funny.

1596
01:03:32.720 --> 01:03:35.640
Then the IOC saying they never read the report

1597
01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:37.520
or they didn't really look into it.

1598
01:03:37.520 --> 01:03:39.480
They didn't vet it enough to realize,

1599
01:03:39.480 --> 01:03:42.410
like anybody who's been to Tokyo in late July

1600
01:03:42.410 --> 01:03:44.070
knows that it's unbearable.

1601
01:03:44.070 --> 01:03:45.890
It's like, would you do the summer Olympics

1602
01:03:45.890 --> 01:03:47.300
in Bushwick in August?

1603
01:03:47.300 --> 01:03:48.823
No, I wouldn't go to that.

1604
01:03:50.441 --> 01:03:52.850
Maybe July, they did the U.S. Open,

1605
01:03:52.850 --> 01:03:54.270
when did they do the U.S. Open?

1606
01:03:54.270 --> 01:03:55.440
Early September.

1607
01:03:55.440 --> 01:03:56.273
Right?

1608
01:03:56.273 --> 01:03:59.978
Yeah, that's when you should be doing in the humid areas,

1609
01:03:59.978 --> 01:04:02.150
the Olympics.

1610
01:04:02.150 --> 01:04:03.090
So, there's that,

1611
01:04:03.090 --> 01:04:05.333
what are their big athletes stories before we get into--

1612
01:04:05.333 --> 01:04:07.930
I mean, there's so many, obviously,

1613
01:04:07.930 --> 01:04:11.284
me being a swimmer, swimming is what I care about the most

1614
01:04:11.284 --> 01:04:12.117
and what I follow

1615
01:04:12.117 --> 01:04:15.590
to the extent that I was able to follow much of it.

1616
01:04:15.590 --> 01:04:18.250
And even then, this is the first Olympics

1617
01:04:18.250 --> 01:04:22.460
where I just really don't have personal relationships

1618
01:04:22.460 --> 01:04:25.200
with any of the athletes or the coaches.

1619
01:04:25.200 --> 01:04:27.610
I think maybe one or two of the coaches I've met in passing,

1620
01:04:27.610 --> 01:04:29.930
but typically, like I have friends

1621
01:04:29.930 --> 01:04:32.750
or people that I know quite well who are competing

1622
01:04:32.750 --> 01:04:34.260
or coaching.

1623
01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:36.190
This year I really didn't have that connection.

1624
01:04:36.190 --> 01:04:39.050
So, it made it less, you know, like I'm so busy.

1625
01:04:39.050 --> 01:04:40.650
It just was like, oh, who are these people?

1626
01:04:40.650 --> 01:04:42.192
I had to like learn who everyone was.

1627
01:04:42.192 --> 01:04:45.220
With a few exceptions, I mean, I think,

1628
01:04:45.220 --> 01:04:48.630
listen, this is the first in five Olympiads

1629
01:04:48.630 --> 01:04:51.310
where there's no Lochte or Phelps.

1630
01:04:51.310 --> 01:04:55.160
I mean, Katie Ledecky is the legacy of that era,

1631
01:04:55.160 --> 01:04:57.143
but you're going into a new,

1632
01:04:58.500 --> 01:05:00.680
it's a new generation of swimmers.

1633
01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:03.930
And that's talk about a heavy burden to live up to

1634
01:05:03.930 --> 01:05:05.360
without those guys around.

1635
01:05:05.360 --> 01:05:08.740
How are you gonna basically put points up on the board

1636
01:05:08.740 --> 01:05:11.880
that are gonna make people interested in what you're doing?

1637
01:05:11.880 --> 01:05:13.020
And with that,

1638
01:05:13.020 --> 01:05:16.174
Caeleb Dressel like showed up five goals, crushed it,

1639
01:05:16.174 --> 01:05:19.180
basically had the ultimate Olympics

1640
01:05:19.180 --> 01:05:21.700
that you could ever expect from that guy.

1641
01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:24.000
And it was just a pleasure watching him swim

1642
01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:27.010
and he set a world record in the 100 fly, right?

1643
01:05:27.010 --> 01:05:28.940
And also on the 4 by 100 medley relay.

1644
01:05:28.940 --> 01:05:29.930
So, that was cool.

1645
01:05:29.930 --> 01:05:31.601
And maybe for crying on the air.

1646
01:05:31.601 --> 01:05:34.240
Yeah, he did cry, he was emotional.

1647
01:05:34.240 --> 01:05:35.130
He's very handsome,

1648
01:05:35.130 --> 01:05:37.840
Leslie Jones has a crush on him, that's important.

1649
01:05:37.840 --> 01:05:38.673
Who doesn't?

1650
01:05:38.673 --> 01:05:41.240
How awesome is Leslie Jones and her Olympic commentary.

1651
01:05:41.240 --> 01:05:42.073
She's hilarious.

1652
01:05:42.073 --> 01:05:44.060
I know, she's so good.

1653
01:05:44.060 --> 01:05:47.020
And they tried to like mimic that

1654
01:05:47.020 --> 01:05:50.670
with Snoop Dogg and Kevin Hart,

1655
01:05:50.670 --> 01:05:53.520
which I felt was a little canned compared to her,

1656
01:05:53.520 --> 01:05:54.424
just very authentic, spontaneous.

1657
01:05:54.424 --> 01:05:57.413
She's like eating snacks while watching television.

1658
01:05:57.413 --> 01:05:59.070
Yeah, it's the best.

1659
01:05:59.070 --> 01:06:02.080
If you're not onto Leslie Jones and her Olympic commentary,

1660
01:06:02.080 --> 01:06:04.613
you gotta follow her on Twitter, she's like amazing.

1661
01:06:05.480 --> 01:06:08.760
So, yeah, I mean, Caeleb Dressel was amazing,

1662
01:06:08.760 --> 01:06:11.140
and it's interesting watching Katie Ladecky,

1663
01:06:11.140 --> 01:06:12.740
like I don't want to belabor all of this.

1664
01:06:12.740 --> 01:06:13.573
There's other places

1665
01:06:13.573 --> 01:06:15.490
where you can get a lot of Olympic feedback,

1666
01:06:15.490 --> 01:06:19.657
but I think that back to this issue of mental health

1667
01:06:19.657 --> 01:06:21.120
and the Olympics,

1668
01:06:21.120 --> 01:06:23.990
we can't discount the fact that all of these athletes

1669
01:06:23.990 --> 01:06:25.300
had to weather a COVID year

1670
01:06:25.300 --> 01:06:27.270
where their training was turned upside down,

1671
01:06:27.270 --> 01:06:28.909
where the predictability

1672
01:06:28.909 --> 01:06:31.500
of whether they would be competing or not

1673
01:06:31.500 --> 01:06:32.930
was very much in question,

1674
01:06:32.930 --> 01:06:37.930
like incredibly difficult when you understand

1675
01:06:38.940 --> 01:06:42.670
that at that level, to compete at the highest level,

1676
01:06:42.670 --> 01:06:45.240
to be that elite, you really have to know

1677
01:06:45.240 --> 01:06:46.770
like here's what I'm competing,

1678
01:06:46.770 --> 01:06:48.220
here's how I'm organizing my schedule

1679
01:06:48.220 --> 01:06:49.670
to be ready by that time.

1680
01:06:49.670 --> 01:06:51.910
And when all those dates are in flux,

1681
01:06:51.910 --> 01:06:54.760
and at least I know in swimming, like pools aren't open,

1682
01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:55.593
then they're open.

1683
01:06:55.593 --> 01:06:58.080
But then it's like, there's a lot of question marks.

1684
01:06:58.080 --> 01:06:59.580
And I think Katie being closer

1685
01:06:59.580 --> 01:07:02.403
to a later phase of her career,

1686
01:07:04.150 --> 01:07:06.610
I think it took a toll on her performance.

1687
01:07:06.610 --> 01:07:07.884
I think if the Olympics had,

1688
01:07:07.884 --> 01:07:09.910
if there had been no coronavirus

1689
01:07:09.910 --> 01:07:11.245
and the Olympics occurred last summer,

1690
01:07:11.245 --> 01:07:13.390
I think she would have swept her events

1691
01:07:13.390 --> 01:07:15.040
and would have been unchallenged.

1692
01:07:17.151 --> 01:07:19.220
But she did get better as it went along, right?

1693
01:07:19.220 --> 01:07:20.150
She did, yeah.

1694
01:07:20.150 --> 01:07:25.150
There was that, when they got the silver in the relay,

1695
01:07:25.860 --> 01:07:26.810
which one was that?

1696
01:07:28.834 --> 01:07:31.376
It was the 4 by 200.

1697
01:07:31.376 --> 01:07:32.210
Was it 4 by 200?

1698
01:07:32.210 --> 01:07:33.411
Yeah, I think so, I think so.

1699
01:07:33.411 --> 01:07:34.320
I don't think I watched that race.

1700
01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:35.790
And they became in silver.

1701
01:07:35.790 --> 01:07:37.350
So, I think she got the silver,

1702
01:07:37.350 --> 01:07:39.973
she got two silvers and two golds, right?

1703
01:07:41.850 --> 01:07:45.035
And in that race, they were far behind.

1704
01:07:45.035 --> 01:07:47.190
Like when she got into the pool,

1705
01:07:47.190 --> 01:07:48.730
she was over a body length behind.

1706
01:07:48.730 --> 01:07:50.460
I thought she was like 10 feet,

1707
01:07:50.460 --> 01:07:52.620
maybe two body lengths behind.

1708
01:07:52.620 --> 01:07:55.600
And to watch her almost reel them all in,

1709
01:07:55.600 --> 01:07:57.600
to me, that was one of the coolest swims I saw

1710
01:07:57.600 --> 01:07:58.480
this entire Olympics.

1711
01:07:58.480 --> 01:07:59.920
I was like you at the beginning,

1712
01:07:59.920 --> 01:08:03.430
the first four or five days, I wasn't paying any attention.

1713
01:08:03.430 --> 01:08:04.630
First of all, it's like,

1714
01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:09.710
by the time you turn on the prime time Olympics,

1715
01:08:09.710 --> 01:08:13.330
you're kind of like everything's already happened.

1716
01:08:13.330 --> 01:08:15.920
And unless you seal yourself from your phone,

1717
01:08:15.920 --> 01:08:17.870
you're gonna know a lot of the results.

1718
01:08:18.900 --> 01:08:21.410
In the past it's been easier for me to manage that.

1719
01:08:21.410 --> 01:08:23.250
Like the last two, London and Rio,

1720
01:08:23.250 --> 01:08:24.980
it's a little bit less of a time zone.

1721
01:08:24.980 --> 01:08:27.280
Problem is still there, but you can manage it.

1722
01:08:28.170 --> 01:08:30.600
In this case, I was like the first four or five days,

1723
01:08:30.600 --> 01:08:31.890
I wasn't paying any attention,

1724
01:08:31.890 --> 01:08:36.850
but I've slowly been wooed back in, even with no crowds.

1725
01:08:36.850 --> 01:08:39.500
It is sad watching the track, especially with the pool,

1726
01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:40.690
you don't see the crowds that much

1727
01:08:40.690 --> 01:08:41.860
'cause you're focused on the pool,

1728
01:08:41.860 --> 01:08:44.960
but the camera angle on the track, it's all the empty seats.

1729
01:08:44.960 --> 01:08:46.222
It is sad.

1730
01:08:46.222 --> 01:08:47.361
It is.

1731
01:08:47.361 --> 01:08:49.110
It's not the way it's supposed to be,

1732
01:08:49.110 --> 01:08:52.240
but I'm seeing Katie Ledecky do that swim.

1733
01:08:52.240 --> 01:08:54.647
And then afterwards, when they were asking her,

1734
01:08:54.647 --> 01:08:55.730
"Is this your last?"

1735
01:08:55.730 --> 01:08:56.827
I think it was her last event.

1736
01:08:56.827 --> 01:08:58.210
"Is this your last swim?"

1737
01:08:58.210 --> 01:08:59.450
And she's like, "I'm not retiring."

1738
01:08:59.450 --> 01:09:03.680
No, she's like, I'll be back in four more years, yeah.

1739
01:09:03.680 --> 01:09:06.000
Which I loved, and I thought she performed great.

1740
01:09:06.000 --> 01:09:09.500
Like again, it goes back to this idea

1741
01:09:09.500 --> 01:09:11.670
is anything but gold a failure.

1742
01:09:11.670 --> 01:09:14.530
And you know, maybe--

1743
01:09:14.530 --> 01:09:16.190
She has a great attitude too.

1744
01:09:16.190 --> 01:09:17.500
Always a smile on her face,

1745
01:09:17.500 --> 01:09:21.020
always a hug, always a nice, positive thing to say.

1746
01:09:21.020 --> 01:09:24.740
Really just acquits herself with such grace.

1747
01:09:24.740 --> 01:09:25.910
What a spectacular athlete.

1748
01:09:25.910 --> 01:09:26.743
I know.

1749
01:09:26.743 --> 01:09:27.576
Yeah.

1750
01:09:27.576 --> 01:09:29.780
Little curious aside.

1751
01:09:29.780 --> 01:09:32.300
So, she's from my club team in D.C.

1752
01:09:32.300 --> 01:09:33.660
She grew up in D.C.

1753
01:09:33.660 --> 01:09:36.500
and she trained when she was in high school

1754
01:09:36.500 --> 01:09:39.453
at the shitty pool at Georgetown Prep,

1755
01:09:40.360 --> 01:09:44.130
this private high school where our club team rents the pool

1756
01:09:44.130 --> 01:09:45.290
for pool time.

1757
01:09:45.290 --> 01:09:48.720
And that's where I trained all through high school

1758
01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:49.960
twice a day.

1759
01:09:49.960 --> 01:09:52.580
And I think they renovated it in the years

1760
01:09:52.580 --> 01:09:54.810
'cause obviously I'm like twice her age or whatever.

1761
01:09:54.810 --> 01:09:56.870
You guys both peed the same pool.

1762
01:09:56.870 --> 01:09:58.160
We both, yes.

1763
01:09:58.160 --> 01:10:00.470
Separated by a decade, probably.

1764
01:10:00.470 --> 01:10:02.460
I think the pool was renovated since I was there,

1765
01:10:02.460 --> 01:10:05.940
but this pool was just a toxic hazard.

1766
01:10:05.940 --> 01:10:10.270
I mean, it would drip like plaque tar from the ceiling

1767
01:10:10.270 --> 01:10:13.170
into it, and we would be like, if we get cancer

1768
01:10:13.170 --> 01:10:14.400
is probably 'cause we're drinking

1769
01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:16.500
whatever's dripping out of the ceiling into the water,

1770
01:10:16.500 --> 01:10:18.760
and you would go in there in the morning

1771
01:10:18.760 --> 01:10:23.390
and it would just be this cloud of chlorine vapor.

1772
01:10:23.390 --> 01:10:25.610
And because we shared it,

1773
01:10:25.610 --> 01:10:28.240
it was belonged to this high school,

1774
01:10:28.240 --> 01:10:30.760
we couldn't use the locker rooms half the time

1775
01:10:30.760 --> 01:10:32.790
because their students, we would like,

1776
01:10:32.790 --> 01:10:34.470
sometimes you could use the football locker rooms

1777
01:10:34.470 --> 01:10:35.970
and go in there, it's all muddy.

1778
01:10:35.970 --> 01:10:37.370
But for afternoon workout,

1779
01:10:37.370 --> 01:10:39.550
we would have to change in the pump room.

1780
01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:43.970
Like can you even get, it was just dirty and disgusting,

1781
01:10:43.970 --> 01:10:46.040
but kind of awesome to that

1782
01:10:46.040 --> 01:10:48.140
like you don't want the super nice pool

1783
01:10:48.140 --> 01:10:49.670
when you're grinding.

1784
01:10:49.670 --> 01:10:51.120
It's like the original Rocky,

1785
01:10:53.264 --> 01:10:57.710
you wanna be in the grimy gritty situation

1786
01:10:57.710 --> 01:11:01.540
so that when you finally qualify for that fancy mean

1787
01:11:01.540 --> 01:11:02.640
and you go to Indianapolis

1788
01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:03.947
and you see that incredible natatorium

1789
01:11:03.947 --> 01:11:05.987
and you're like, "Wow," right?

1790
01:11:05.987 --> 01:11:08.860
But you don't wanna train in that fancy place every day.

1791
01:11:08.860 --> 01:11:11.190
You guys are like the Hickory, like in Hoosiers.

1792
01:11:11.190 --> 01:11:12.560
You're like the team Hickory.

1793
01:11:12.560 --> 01:11:14.540
The word they makes me very uncomfortable.

1794
01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:15.373
Like I should not,

1795
01:11:15.373 --> 01:11:18.220
like I'm just saying I swam in the same pool as her,

1796
01:11:18.220 --> 01:11:19.550
like I don't know...

1797
01:11:19.550 --> 01:11:22.120
Good for Katie, she's the all-time GOAT.

1798
01:11:22.120 --> 01:11:23.404
Unbelievable athlete.

1799
01:11:23.404 --> 01:11:26.720
Shout out GW Preps' horrible pool.

1800
01:11:26.720 --> 01:11:27.553
I know.

1801
01:11:27.553 --> 01:11:29.470
Nice, how about Emma McKeon?

1802
01:11:29.470 --> 01:11:32.572
Australian medalist, seven medals.

1803
01:11:32.572 --> 01:11:36.400
Winningest female Australian swimmer ever, I think.

1804
01:11:36.400 --> 01:11:37.453
Yep incredible.

1805
01:11:37.453 --> 01:11:40.120
I think the winningest swimmer.

1806
01:11:40.120 --> 01:11:40.953
I mean--

1807
01:11:40.953 --> 01:11:42.820
Seven medals, four gold.

1808
01:11:42.820 --> 01:11:43.670
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1809
01:11:43.670 --> 01:11:44.503
So, the most medals.

1810
01:11:44.503 --> 01:11:46.480
Very few have gotten that.

1811
01:11:46.480 --> 01:11:49.040
I think, well, Caeleb got five gold

1812
01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:51.860
and the only other swimmers to do that are Phelps

1813
01:11:51.860 --> 01:11:54.260
and Mark Spitz and--

1814
01:11:54.260 --> 01:11:55.093
Biondi.

1815
01:11:55.093 --> 01:11:56.140
Biondi in '88, right?

1816
01:11:56.140 --> 01:11:57.350
In Seoul.

1817
01:11:57.350 --> 01:11:59.150
